  urldate = {2022-06-11},
  abstract = {Perovskite solar cells (PSCs) are promising to reduce the cost of photovoltaic system due to their low-cost raw materials and high-throughput solution process, however it has not been demonstrated yet to fabricate all the active layers in perovskite modules using scalable solution process. Herein, we report the fabrication of highly efficient PSCs and modules in ambient conditions with all layers bladed except the metal electrode by overcoming the challenges of blading of 36\textpm 9 nm thick electron transport layer (ETL) on perovskite films with a roughness of {$\sim$}80 nm. A combination of additives in phenyl-C61-butyric acid methyl ester (PCBM) allows PCBM to conformally cover perovskites and still have a good electrical conductivity. Amine functionalized molecules are added to enhance both the dispersity of PCBM molecules and the affinity to perovskites. A PCBM dopant of 4-(2,3-Dihydro-1,3-dimethyl-1H-benzimidazol-2-yl)-N,N-dimethylbenzenamine (N-DMBI) recovers the conductivity loss induced by small amine functionalized molecules. Perovskite solar cells (0.08 cm2) fabricated by all blading process have reached an average efficiency of {$\sim$}22.4\textpm 0.5\% and a champion efficiency of 23.1\% for perovskites with a bandgap of 1.51 eV, with much better stability compared to evaporated ETL solar cells. The all-bladed minimodule with an area of 25.03 cm2 shows an aperture efficiency of {$\sim$}19.3\%, showing the good uniformity of the bladed ETLs. This article is protected by copyright. All rights reserved},
